{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"iterators","title":"Iterators","group":"STL Containers & Iterators","order":56,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">An iterator is a generalised pointer: it points to an element, <code>*it</code> reads it, <code>++it</code> moves on. Iterators are the glue that lets any algorithm run on any container.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Basics</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;iterator&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{10, 20, 30, 40};\n    for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) std::cout &lt;&lt; *it &lt;&lt; ' ';   // [begin, end)\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    for (auto it = v.rbegin(); it != v.rend(); ++it) std::cout &lt;&lt; *it &lt;&lt; ' '; // reverse\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    auto it = v.begin() + 2;                    // random access\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *it &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; it[1] &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (it - v.begin()) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *std::next(v.begin()) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; *std::prev(v.end()) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::distance(v.begin(), v.end()) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    for (auto cit = v.cbegin(); cit != v.cend(); ++cit) { /* *cit = 0; would not compile */ }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 20 30 40\n40 30 20 10\n30 40 2\n20 40 4</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Half-open ranges</div><p><code>end()</code> points <strong>one past</strong> the last element — it marks the end and must never be dereferenced. An empty container has <code>begin() == end()</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stairs\"></i>Iterator categories</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Supports</th><th>Example containers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Input / Output</td><td>Single pass: <code>++</code>, <code>*</code> (read or write)</td><td><code>istream_iterator</code> / <code>ostream_iterator</code>, <code>back_inserter</code></td></tr><tr><td>Forward</td><td>Multi-pass <code>++</code></td><td><code>forward_list</code>, <code>unordered_*</code></td></tr><tr><td>Bidirectional</td><td>+ <code>--</code></td><td><code>list</code>, <code>set</code>, <code>map</code></td></tr><tr><td>Random access</td><td>+ <code>it + n</code>, <code>it[n]</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>it2 - it1</code></td><td><code>deque</code></td></tr><tr><td>Contiguous (C++20)</td><td>+ elements adjacent in memory</td><td><code>vector</code>, <code>array</code>, <code>string</code>, raw arrays</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Algorithms state what they need: <code>std::sort</code> requires random access (so <code>std::list</code> has its own <code>sort()</code>), <code>std::reverse</code> needs bidirectional, <code>std::find</code> just input.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plug\"></i>Iterator adaptors</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;iterator&gt;\n#include &lt;sstream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::istringstream in(\"5 3 8 1\");\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v(std::istream_iterator&lt;int&gt;{in}, std::istream_iterator&lt;int&gt;{});   // read all ints\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; evens;\n    std::copy_if(v.begin(), v.end(), std::back_inserter(evens), [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });\n\n    std::copy(v.begin(), v.end(), std::ostream_iterator&lt;int&gt;(std::cout, \",\"));   // print with separator\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \" evens=\" &lt;&lt; evens.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5,3,8,1, evens=1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>Making your own class iterable</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nclass Range {                                   // like Python's range(lo, hi)\n    int lo_, hi_;\npublic:\n    Range(int lo, int hi) : lo_(lo), hi_(hi) {}\n    struct iterator {\n        int cur;\n        int operator*() const { return cur; }\n        iterator&amp; operator++() { ++cur; return *this; }\n        bool operator==(const iterator&amp;) const = default;\n    };\n    iterator begin() const { return {lo_}; }\n    iterator end() const { return {hi_}; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    for (int i : Range(3, 7)) std::cout &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 4 5 6</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>An iterator is only valid while its container isn't restructured: e.g. a <code>vector</code> reallocation or erasing the element it points to invalidates it. Using an invalidated iterator is undefined behaviour.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does the STL use half-open ranges [begin, end)?</summary><div><p>An empty range is simply <code>begin == end</code>, the length is <code>end - begin</code>, loops are <code>while (it != end)</code>, and ranges split cleanly: <code>[a, m)</code> and <code>[m, b)</code> share no element and miss none. It also allows <code>end()</code> for containers where a “last element” pointer would be awkward.</p></div></details></section>"}